 [`/no-comments`](../../../no-comments/SKILL.md) spawns [Comment Sicko](../../../no-comments/references/comment-sicko.md), an independent reviewer that may delete comments but not edit application code with a short keep list: license headers, doc comments on a public API, links that explain what code can't, behavior forced by an external dependency you can't reshape. Everything else goes. A surprise in your own code gets no such pass. The comment comes back as a refactor flag, and `/no-comments` fixes the flags it accepts at the root cause. When a comment claims a constraint, "do not remove", the skill offers to encode the claim as a type, test, or lint. Either way, the comment comes out.
 
-The division of labor is worth keeping straight. Code cleanup removes slop from the diff, `/unslop` cleans it out of prose, and `/no-comments` hands the comments to a reviewer who didn't write them.
+`/deslop` cleans code, `/unslop` cleans prose, and `/no-comments` hands the comments to a reviewer who didn't write them.
 
 **Pitfall:** cleanup is not optional polish. A diff with narrating comments and defensive dead weight reads as unfinished to reviewers, and the extra code is where the next bug hides. If the diff feels padded, say `deslop it` before you commit, not after review calls it out.
 
